==English==
{{was wotd|2008|July|25}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|LL.|assimilātus}}, variant of {{der|en|la|assimulātus||made similar, imitated}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|assimulō}}, from {{m|la|ad}} + {{m|la|simulō||imitate, copy}}. {{doublet|en|assemble}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/əˈsɪm.ɪ.leɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-assimilate.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-assimilate.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|assimilat}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[incorporate]] [[nutrient]]s into the [[body]], especially after [[digestion]].
#: ''Food is '''assimilated''' and converted into organic tissue.''
#* Isaac Newton
#*: Hence also animals and vegetables may '''assimilate''' their nourishment.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To incorporate or [[absorb]] ([[knowledge]]) into the [[mind]].
#: ''The teacher paused in her lecture to allow the students to '''assimilate''' what she had said.''
#* Merivale
#*: His mind had no power to '''assimilate''' the lessons.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To absorb (a person or people) into a [[community]] or [[culture]].
#: ''The aliens in the science-fiction film wanted to '''assimilate''' human beings into their own race.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[compare]] to something [[similar]]. {{rfex|en}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring to a likeness or to conformity; to cause a resemblance between.
#* John Bright
#*: to '''assimilate''' our law to the law of Scotland
#* Cowper
#*: Fast falls a fleecy shower; the downy flakes / '''Assimilate''' all objects.
#: {{rfquotek|en|Sir M. Hale}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become similar.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be incorporated or absorbed into something.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|incorporate or absorb knowledge into the mind}} {{l|en|process}}
* {{sense|absorb a group of people into a community}} {{l|en|integrate}}

